---

# Decision Hierarchy

When objectives conflict, the following hierarchy always applies.

Safety

↓

Health

↓

Recovery

↓

Long-Term Adaptation

↓

Performance

↓

Convenience

This hierarchy is immutable.

No module may override it.

---

# Decision Cycle

Every recommendation follows six stages.

Stage 1

Collect observations.

Stage 2

Interpret observations.

Stage 3

Generate candidate strategies.

Stage 4

Evaluate candidates.

Stage 5

Select the optimal strategy.

Stage 6

Generate explanation.

---

# Candidate Strategies

The engine never evaluates a single option.

Instead it generates several possible approaches.

Example:

Strategy A

Heavy Strength Session

Strategy B

Reduced Volume Session

Strategy C

Technical Session

Strategy D

Recovery Session

Each strategy is evaluated.

The best one is selected.

---

# Strategy Evaluation

Each candidate receives scores for:

Safety

Recovery

Specificity

Expected Adaptation

Long-Term Benefit

Confidence

Athlete Preference

Competition Relevance

No single score determines the outcome.

---

# Decision Confidence

Every decision carries confidence.

Very High

High

Moderate

Low

Unknown

Confidence reflects information quality.

Not expected performance.

---

# Conflict Resolution

Different engines may disagree.

Example

Recovery Engine

Reduce volume.

Progression Engine

Increase load.

Safety Engine

Maintain current load.

The Decision Engine resolves these conflicts.

Conflict resolution must always be explainable.

---

# Athlete Goals

Goals influence priorities.

Examples

Strength

Explosiveness

Power Endurance

Competition Preparation

Weight Management

General Performance

Different goals produce different optimal decisions.

---

# Training Phase

The Decision Engine adapts according to:

Preparation Phase

Development Phase

Competition Phase

Peak

Deload

Transition

The same recovery state may produce different decisions depending on the current phase.

---

# Sport Specificity

Combat athletes require different decisions than endurance athletes.

Examples

Heavy Bag

Sparring

Footwork

Grip

Power

Explosive Striking

Reactive Movement

Decision priorities remain sport-specific.

---

# Constraints

The Decision Engine considers:

Time available.

Equipment.

Location.

Injury.

Travel.

Recovery.

Schedule.

Environmental conditions.

Constraints reduce the decision space.

They do not reduce decision quality.

---

# Decision Stability

Recommendations should not fluctuate unnecessarily.

Small variations in input should rarely produce large variations in output.

Consistency builds trust.

---

# Adaptation

The Decision Engine learns.

It continuously updates:

Athlete response.

Recovery behaviour.

Progression tolerance.

Exercise preferences.

Compliance.

Future recommendations become increasingly individualized.

---

# Explainability

Every decision must be explainable.

The engine stores:

Decision.

Evidence.

Applied rules.

Alternative strategies.

Confidence.

Expected outcome.

The Explainability Engine converts this into athlete-friendly language.

---

# Human Override

The athlete may reject recommendations.

The Decision Engine records:

Recommendation.

Athlete decision.

Reason (when available).

Future recommendations may adapt.

The athlete always retains final authority.

---

# Safety Override

Safety recommendations cannot be ignored internally.

If the Safety Engine detects unacceptable risk,

the Decision Engine must select a safer strategy.

Safety always overrides performance.

---

# Conservative Behaviour

When uncertainty increases,

the Decision Engine becomes more conservative.

Unknown information never justifies more aggressive programming.

---

# Failure Handling

Unexpected outcomes trigger reassessment.

Examples

Repeated missed repetitions.

Unexpected fatigue.

Pain.

Illness.

Rapid performance decline.

The engine investigates before recommending progression.

---

